{
  "stage": "verify",
  "item": "ch03.s11",
  "inputs_hash": "3820f47b7a431473a217344dfa3799f937a6683c7dd805c41fe8e5f6da49b760",
  "output_hash": "1b22788e9a067f849f234ec61ab27cc1c60f572803d03bfbc79c4a6928427ec2",
  "output": {
    "section_id": "ch03.s11",
    "paragraphs": [
      {
        "id": "ch03.s11.p01",
        "sentences": [
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p01.01",
            "text": "Why do transformer blocks include those extra arrows and plus signs, and why do they matter for training stability?",
            "type": "C",
            "cites": []
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p01.02",
            "text": "Shortcut connections are an important building block of the GPT architecture alongside layer normalization, the GELU activation, and the feed-forward neural network.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "2r0QahNdwMw@23",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@28",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@30",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@32",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@35",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1775",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1784"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p01.03",
            "text": "In a Transformer block, the plus symbols and their associated arrows represent shortcut connections.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "2r0QahNdwMw@117",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@122",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@124",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@130",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@132",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1877",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1879"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p01.04",
            "text": "Shortcut connections are also known as skip connections or residual connections.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "2r0QahNdwMw@157",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@158",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@160",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@162",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@164"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p01.05",
            "text": "They were first proposed in computer vision to solve the vanishing gradient problem.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "2r0QahNdwMw@167",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@172",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@174",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@178"
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "ch03.s11.p02",
        "sentences": [
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p02.01",
            "text": "The vanishing gradient problem is that gradients become progressively smaller as they are propagated backward through a neural network.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "2r0QahNdwMw@194",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@195",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@198",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@200"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p02.02",
            "text": "During backpropagation, gradient flow goes from the output of the network to the input, so later-layer gradients are used to compute earlier-layer gradients.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "2r0QahNdwMw@252",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@254",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@256",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@258",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@265",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@267",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@269",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@271",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@273"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p02.03",
            "text": "Because backpropagation involves multiplications, if a later gradient becomes small then earlier gradients become even smaller, which is why gradients can vanish.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "2r0QahNdwMw@273",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@275",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@277",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@279",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@282",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@286",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@288",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@290",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@292",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@295",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@299",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@302",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@304",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@306"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p02.04",
            "text": "When gradients become very small, weight updates are not made, learning becomes stagnant, convergence is delayed, and the language model does not learn well.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "2r0QahNdwMw@200",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@202",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@205",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@207",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@210",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@212"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p02.05",
            "text": "The weight update rule is that the new weight equals the old weight minus the step size times the partial derivative of the loss with respect to the weight.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "2r0QahNdwMw@324",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@327",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@329",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@331",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@333",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@336",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@339"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p02.06",
            "text": "If the gradient of the loss with respect to a weight approaches zero, the weight is not updated, so the neural network is not learning.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "2r0QahNdwMw@349",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@351",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@353",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@355",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@357",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@362",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@364",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@366",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@368",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@370",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@373",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@375",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@376"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p02.07",
            "text": "This leads to stagnation, and shortcut connections solve that problem.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "2r0QahNdwMw@378",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@381",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@385",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@388",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@391",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@393"
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "ch03.s11.p03",
        "sentences": [
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p03.r01",
            "text": "A shortcut connection creates an alternative path for the gradient to flow by skipping one or more layers.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "2r0QahNdwMw@398",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@401",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@403",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@407"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p03.r02",
            "text": "It is implemented by adding the output of one layer to the output of a later layer.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "2r0QahNdwMw@412",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@415",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@512",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@515"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p03.r03",
            "text": "If the earlier output is y_l and the later layer computes f(y_l), then with a shortcut connection the later output becomes f(y_l) + y_l.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "2r0QahNdwMw@602",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@604",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@606",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@610",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@620",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@622",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@624",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@626",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@629",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@630"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p03.r04",
            "text": "To prevent vanishing gradients, the partial derivative of the loss with respect to the first-layer output should stay as large as possible so that the gradients with respect to the first-layer weights do not go to zero.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "2r0QahNdwMw@687",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@688",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@701",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@703",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@705",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@707",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@709",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@711",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@713",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@715",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@717"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p03.r05",
            "text": "Using the chain rule, the partial derivative of the loss with respect to y_l equals the partial derivative of the loss with respect to y_(l+1) multiplied by the partial derivative of y_(l+1) with respect to y_l.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "2r0QahNdwMw@724",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@725",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@728",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@729",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@732",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@734"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p03.r06",
            "text": "Because y_(l+1) = f(y_l) + y_l, the derivative of y_(l+1) with respect to y_l includes the derivative of f(y_l) plus 1.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "2r0QahNdwMw@739",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@741",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@743",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@746",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@748"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p03.r07",
            "text": "That plus-one term keeps the gradient from vanishing even if the derivative through the main path becomes small.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "2r0QahNdwMw@767",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@770",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@776",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@780",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@785",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@788",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@790",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@793",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@795",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@799",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@800",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@802",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@804",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@806"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p03.r08",
            "text": "Because the shortcut connection keeps gradients flowing, the loss gradients with respect to earlier-layer weights do not ultimately vanish, so we avoid stagnation in the weight updates.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "2r0QahNdwMw@809",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@811",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@813",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@815",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@818",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@820",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@822",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@824",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@826",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@828",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@830",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@832",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@835",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@839",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@841",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@843",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@845",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@847"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p03.r09",
            "text": "Shortcut connections therefore play a crucial role during backpropagation by preserving gradient flow through the network.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "2r0QahNdwMw@878",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@879",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@882",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@884",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@887"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p03.r10",
            "text": "They also smooth the loss-function landscape and make gradient flow smoother.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "2r0QahNdwMw@925",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@927",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@928",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@931",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@946",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@948",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@949",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@952"
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "ch03.s11.p04",
        "sentences": [
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p04.01",
            "text": "A small code example makes this effect concrete by stacking multiple layers, optionally adding shortcut connections, and then comparing gradient-flow magnitudes with and without shortcuts.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "2r0QahNdwMw@966",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@967",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@969",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@976",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@979",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@981",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@984",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@991",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@993",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@996",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@998",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1000"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p04.02",
            "text": "The argument \"layer_sizes\" specifies how many neurons are in each layer.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1016",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1018",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1021"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p04.03",
            "text": "If \"layer_sizes\" is \"3, 3, 3, 3, 1\", the network has five layers with three neurons and a final layer with one neuron.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1023",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1026",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1029",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1030",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1033",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1036",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1038",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1040"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p04.04",
            "text": "The argument \"use_shortcut\" determines whether shortcut connections are used: \"false\" disables them and \"true\" enables them.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1047",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1049",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1051",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1053",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1055"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p04.05",
            "text": "PyTorch's \"nn.Sequential\" module connects different neural network layers together.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1057",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1059",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1064",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1066",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1069",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1070"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p04.06",
            "text": "The network is constructed by chaining layers so that one entry of \"layer_sizes\" gives the input dimension, the next entry gives the output dimension, and each layer uses a GELU activation function.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1080",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1083",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1084",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1087",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1088",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1091",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1094",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1096",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1097",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1099",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1236",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1238"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p04.07",
            "text": "For a layer, the input dimension is determined by the previous layer's output size and the output dimension is determined by the next layer's size.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1145",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1146",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1148",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1150",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1152",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1194",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1198",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1200",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1202",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1206",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1207",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1210",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1212",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1214",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1217",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1220",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1222"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p04.08",
            "text": "The code uses the GELU activation function.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1088",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1099",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1236",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1238"
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "ch03.s11.p05",
        "sentences": [
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p05.r01",
            "text": "In the forward method, first consider the case without shortcut connections.",
            "type": "C",
            "cites": []
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p05.r02",
            "text": "Without shortcut connections, the output of each layer is fed to the next layer until the final output is produced.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1250",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1253",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1255",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1259",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1261",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1263",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1265",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1267",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1269",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1271"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p05.r03",
            "text": "With shortcut connections, each step adds the current layer output to the running value from previous steps, so the final result accumulates all earlier shortcut additions.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1274",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1276",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1280",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1282",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1286",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1289",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1292",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1294",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1296",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1302",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1304",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1308",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1310",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1317",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1319",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1321",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1325",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1327",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1331",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1333",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1335",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1337",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1341",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1344"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p05.r04",
            "text": "The shortcut connection itself is implemented by a single line that updates \"x\" to \"x\" plus the layer output.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1349",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1350"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p05.r05",
            "text": "The implemented deep neural network has five layers, each consisting of a linear layer and a GELU activation, and the forward pass optionally adds shortcut connections when the \"use_shortcut\" attribute is true.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1354",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1355",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1358",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1360",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1363",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1365",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1366",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1368",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1371",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1372"
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "ch03.s11.p06",
        "sentences": [
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p06.r01",
            "text": "To inspect gradient flow, the example initializes a neural network without shortcut connections, uses layer sizes 3 3 3 3 3 1, takes a three-dimensional sample input with values 1, 0, and minus 1, and sets use shortcut to false.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1375",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1377",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1383",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1385",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1388",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1390",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1393",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1417",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1421",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1423",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1426",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1428"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p06.r02",
            "text": "In layers with input dimension 3 and output dimension 3, each neuron has three weights, so the weight matrix is 3 by 3, while the final layer has a 3 by 1 weight matrix.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1457",
