              "2r0QahNdwMw@1472",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1474",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1476",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1478",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1480",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1482"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p06.r03",
            "text": "To summarize gradient flow per layer, the backward pass computes gradients for all weights in a layer and then takes the mean of those values as that layer's mean gradient.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1482",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1484",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1487",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1489",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1491",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1494",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1495",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1498",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1500",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1501",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1503",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1506",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1508",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1510",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1513",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1515"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p06.r04",
            "text": "The loss is defined as the squared difference between the model output y and the ground truth, for example (y - 0) squared when the ground truth is zero.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1527",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1530",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1532",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1534",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1538"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p06.r05",
            "text": "In backpropagation, the partial derivatives of the loss are computed starting from the output layer and then moving backward through earlier layers until the first layer.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1549",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1551",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1552",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1554",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1556",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1557",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1559",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1561",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1562",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1565"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p06.r06",
            "text": "The code sets the target to zero, computes the model output for x, uses a squared loss, calls loss.backward(), and prints a single gradient value per layer as the mean absolute gradient of that layer’s 3 by 3 values.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1566",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1568",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1570",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1573",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1574",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1576",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1578",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1580",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1584",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1587",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1589",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1630",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1633",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1635",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1637"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p06.r07",
            "text": "In PyTorch, the backward method conveniently computes loss gradients during the backward pass.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1640",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1642",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1644",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1646"
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "ch03.s11.p07",
        "sentences": [
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p07.r01",
            "text": "Without shortcut connections, the mean gradient becomes much smaller in earlier layers, illustrating the vanishing gradient problem.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1658",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1659",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1662",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1665",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1668",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1670",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1672",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1675",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1676",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1679",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1681",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1683",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1685"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p07.r02",
            "text": "Adding shortcut connections creates alternative paths for gradients to flow between layers.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1698",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1700",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1702",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1705",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1707",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1709",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1711",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1713",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1715",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1718",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1881",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1883"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p07.r03",
            "text": "With shortcut connections, the earlier-layer gradients remain much larger and do not become negligible, showing that shortcut connections solve the vanishing gradient problem in this example.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1722",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1724",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1726",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1729",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1730",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1733",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1736",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1738",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1740",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1743",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1745",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1748",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1751",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1753",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1755"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p07.r04",
            "text": "With shortcut connections, the gradient values stabilize toward the first layer instead of shrinking to a vanishingly small value.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1757",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1759",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1761",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1763"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p07.r05",
            "text": "Shortcut connections are an important way to overcome the vanishing gradient problem in deep neural networks.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1775",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1777",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1779",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1781"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p07.r06",
            "text": "They are a core building block of large language models and help training by ensuring consistent gradient flow across layers in GPT models.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1784",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1786",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1788",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1791",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1793"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p07.r07",
            "text": "They help stabilize training.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1833",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1835",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1836",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1839",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1841"
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "ch03.s11.p08",
        "sentences": [
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p08.01",
            "text": "Returning to the Transformer block, the reason for those arrows is now clear.",
            "type": "C",
            "cites": []
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p08.02",
            "text": "The arrows between layers are shortcut connections, and they provide an alternative path for gradients to flow.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1872",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1875",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1877",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1879",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1881",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1883"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p08.03",
            "text": "A shortcut connection can be added between layers of the Transformer block to help solve the vanishing gradient problem.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1885",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1886",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1888",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1890"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p08.04",
            "text": "Because backpropagation passes through the entire Transformer block from output to input, the architecture must ensure that gradients do not vanish so that learning remains stable.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1893",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1895",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1896",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1900",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1902",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1905"
            ]
          }
        ]
      }
